---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../../../components/Header.astro';
import Hero from '../../../components/Hero.astro';
import Footer from '../../../components/Footer.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="The agent — Make It So Camp Tokyo"
  description="The Make It So agent breaks one piece of your work into goal, subgoals and activities, then decides with you, activity by activity, what an agent takes over and where a person stays in."
  canonicalPath="/new/tokyo/agent/"
  robots="noindex, nofollow"
>
  <Header links={[
    { label: 'Why', href: '#why' },
    { label: 'The process', href: '#process' },
    { label: 'Three moves', href: '#moves' },
    { label: 'What you leave with', href: '#card' },
    { label: 'The camp', href: '/new/tokyo/' },
  ]} />

  <Hero
    leftMeta="The agent"
    rightMeta={'Make It So Camp Tokyo<br><span class="light">in development</span>'}
    statement="Break one piece of your work into goal, subgoals and activities. Then decide, activity by activity, what an agent takes over and where a person stays in."
    small={true}
    minHeight="42svh"
  />

  <section class="section" id="why" aria-labelledby="why-label">
    <div class="section-label"><span class="num">01</span><h2 class="label" id="why-label">Why</h2></div>
    <p class="lead-statement">You cannot delegate what you cannot articulate.</p>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <p>Most of how we work is tacit. We know how to open the jar, so we never write it down, and a description of our work assumes everything a grown-up would know. That is what the sandwich exercise on day one showed: written for a partner who follows the instructions exactly as written, it goes wrong at the steps nobody thought to mention.</p>
        <p>An agent is that partner. It does not share the assumptions, and it does the wrong thing at exactly the step that was left out.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>Asked for a workflow cold, people give the tidy version. The time goes into waiting, chasing, rework and exceptions, and none of that shows up because none of it feels like work. What people can describe with the gaps filled in is last Tuesday.</p>
        <p>The agent is an interview that starts from last Tuesday. It takes one thing you do regularly, finds the last time you did it, and works down from the goal to the activities, one question at a time. Then it asks, activity by activity, where you need to stay in.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="process" aria-labelledby="process-label">
    <div class="section-label"><span class="num">02</span><h2 class="label" id="process-label">The process</h2></div>
    <p class="lead-statement">One piece of work, taken apart.</p>
    <figure class="flow">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 900 520" role="img" aria-label="One piece of work becomes a goal; the goal splits into three subgoals; each subgoal splits into activities; under every activity a dot marks whether it stays with the person or an agent does it; from that, the goal is restated: what you are actually working for, now that you know what you hand off and what you keep. A bracket marks the whole tree as written down, in your words.">
        <defs>
          <marker id="arr" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>
          <marker id="arrA" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" fill="var(--accent)"/></marker>
        </defs>
        <g font-family="var(--sans)" font-size="13">
          <!-- layer labels -->
          <g fill="var(--muted)" font-size="11" letter-spacing="1.6" text-anchor="end">
            <text x="200" y="55">THE WORK</text>
            <text x="200" y="135">GOAL</text>
            <text x="200" y="215">SUBGOALS</text>
            <text x="200" y="295">ACTIVITIES</text>
            <text x="200" y="350">WHO DOES IT</text>
            <text x="200" y="465">GOAL, RESTATED</text>
          </g>

          <!-- the work -->
          <rect x="340" y="30" width="220" height="40" rx="2" fill="var(--bg)" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2"/>
          <text x="450" y="55" text-anchor="middle" fill="currentColor">one thing you do regularly</text>
          <line x1="450" y1="71" x2="450" y2="108" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" marker-end="url(#arr)"/>

          <!-- goal -->
          <rect x="340" y="110" width="220" height="40" rx="2" fill="var(--bg)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
          <text x="450" y="135" text-anchor="middle" fill="currentColor">what it is for</text>
          <line x1="450" y1="151" x2="450" y2="170" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2"/>
          <line x1="300" y1="170" x2="600" y2="170" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2"/>
          <g stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" marker-end="url(#arr)">
            <line x1="300" y1="170" x2="300" y2="188"/><line x1="450" y1="170" x2="450" y2="188"/><line x1="600" y1="170" x2="600" y2="188"/>
          </g>

          <!-- subgoals -->
          <g fill="var(--bg)" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2">
            <rect x="240" y="190" width="120" height="36" rx="2"/><rect x="390" y="190" width="120" height="36" rx="2"/><rect x="540" y="190" width="120" height="36" rx="2"/>
          </g>
          <g fill="var(--muted)" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle">
            <text x="300" y="213">the parts</text><text x="450" y="213">in the order</text><text x="600" y="213">they happen</text>
          </g>
          <!-- subgoal to activities -->
          <g stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" fill="none">
            <path d="M300,227 L300,250 M270,250 L330,250 M270,250 L270,266 M330,250 L330,266"/>
            <path d="M450,227 L450,250 M420,250 L480,250 M420,250 L420,266 M480,250 L480,266"/>
            <path d="M600,227 L600,250 M570,250 L630,250 M570,250 L570,266 M630,250 L630,266"/>
          </g>

          <!-- activities -->
          <g fill="var(--bg)" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1">
            <rect x="246" y="268" width="48" height="34" rx="2"/><rect x="306" y="268" width="48" height="34" rx="2"/>
            <rect x="396" y="268" width="48" height="34" rx="2"/><rect x="456" y="268" width="48" height="34" rx="2"/>
            <rect x="546" y="268" width="48" height="34" rx="2"/><rect x="606" y="268" width="48" height="34" rx="2"/>
          </g>
          <text x="450" y="320" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--muted)" font-size="11">what you actually did, each with what it needs and what it produces</text>

          <!-- who does it -->
          <g stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.6">
            <circle cx="270" cy="345" r="7" fill="var(--accent)"/>
            <circle cx="330" cy="345" r="7" fill="var(--bg)"/>
            <circle cx="420" cy="345" r="7" fill="var(--bg)"/>
            <circle cx="480" cy="345" r="7" fill="var(--accent)"/>
            <circle cx="570" cy="345" r="7" fill="var(--bg)"/>
            <circle cx="630" cy="345" r="7" fill="var(--bg)"/>
          </g>
          <g font-size="12">
            <circle cx="300" cy="380" r="5" fill="var(--accent)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="312" y="384" fill="currentColor">stays with you, for a reason</text>
            <circle cx="500" cy="380" r="5" fill="var(--bg)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="512" y="384" fill="currentColor">an agent does it</text>
          </g>

          <!-- down to the restated goal -->
          <line x1="450" y1="396" x2="450" y2="428" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#arrA)"/>

          <!-- goal, restated -->
          <rect x="280" y="430" width="340" height="64" rx="2" fill="#F2E9E4" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
          <text x="450" y="455" text-anchor="middle" fill="currentColor">what you are actually working for,</text>
          <text x="450" y="473" text-anchor="middle" fill="currentColor">now that you know what you hand off and what you keep</text>

          <!-- the whole tree, written down -->
          <path d="M700,30 L712,30 L712,494 L700,494" stroke="var(--muted)" stroke-width="1" fill="none"/>
          <text x="724" y="258" fill="var(--muted)" font-size="11" letter-spacing="1.6">ALL OF THIS,</text>
          <text x="724" y="272" fill="var(--muted)" font-size="11" letter-spacing="1.6">WRITTEN DOWN,</text>
          <text x="724" y="286" fill="var(--muted)" font-size="11" letter-spacing="1.6">IN YOUR WORDS</text>
        </g>
      </svg>
      <figcaption>One piece of work becomes a goal, the goal becomes subgoals, the subgoals become activities, and every activity is placed with you or with an agent. Knowing which is which, you can say what you are actually working for. The whole tree is your way of working, written down.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="moves" aria-labelledby="moves-label">
    <div class="section-label"><span class="num">03</span><h2 class="label" id="moves-label">Three moves</h2></div>
    <p class="lead-statement">The first three skills on the camp’s list, done with you.</p>
    <div class="moves">
      <div class="move">
        <span class="num">1</span>
        <h3>See it as a workflow</h3>
        <p>What the thing is for, once it is done. Then everything that happened the last time, past tense, in any order, including the waiting and the chasing. Then the order, and the breaks between the parts. The parts are the subgoals, and under each one the activities: what you opened, wrote, sent, checked.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="move">
        <span class="num">2</span>
        <h3>Inputs and outputs</h3>
        <p>For each activity: what you needed to start it, where that came from, what it produced, who got it. A supplier you cannot name is a missing step. An output nobody receives is a step that can go. An input that arrived late is where the time went.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="move">
        <span class="num">3</span>
        <h3>Decide where you stay in</h3>
        <p>Each activity gets a rung on a five-step ladder, from <em>I do it</em> to <em>it does, I never see it</em>. Keeping one needs a reason, and four count: the consequence is hard to reverse; there is no ground truth, only judgment; someone needs to know a person did it; it is where your own signal comes from. Habit does not count.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="card" aria-labelledby="card-label">
    <div class="section-label"><span class="num">04</span><h2 class="label" id="card-label">What you leave with</h2></div>
    <p class="lead-statement">A clearer goal, and the method that gets you there.</p>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <p><strong>Your way of working, written down.</strong> Goal, subgoals, activities with what each needs and produces, a rung and a reason per activity, what stays with you, what gets built. In your words throughout. It is the thing you can hand to any agent afterwards, and the thing a colleague can read and disagree with.</p>
        <p><strong>The goal, restated.</strong> At the start you can only state the goal from inside the work as it is now. At the end, knowing which activities you hand off and which you keep, you can say what you are actually working for. What someone keeps says what they think the work is for, better than anything they said going in. If the two goals match, nothing new was found, which is a fair result. If they differ, the difference is the result.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p><strong>One thing built.</strong> If an activity sits high enough on the ladder, the agent builds it: it takes the activity’s inputs and outputs from the card, asks how you would know the result was good before anything is built, and builds against that. One activity per session. If nothing clears the bar, it says so, and you still leave with the method written down.</p>
        <p><strong>The second pass.</strong> After the built thing has run on a real instance, come back. Automating an activity does not remove it from the work; it changes the work around it, and the tree gets redrawn.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="status" aria-labelledby="status-label">
    <div class="section-label"><span class="num">05</span><h2 class="label" id="status-label">Where it stands</h2></div>
    <p class="lead-statement">In development. <span class="dim">Not yet run on a real participant.</span></p>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <p>Two front ends run the same interview. A Claude Code skill that writes the method to disk and builds one activity from it, and a prototype connector for Claude that fills the tree in on screen as you talk.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>Questions: <a href="mailto:hello@misocamp.com">hello@misocamp.com</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .flow { margin:clamp(2rem, 3.5vw, 3rem) 0 0; padding:clamp(1rem, 2vw, 1.5rem); border:1px solid var(--line); overflow-x:auto; color:var(--ink); }
  .flow svg { display:block; width:100%; max-width:900px; margin:0 auto; height:auto; min-width:640px; }
  .flow figcaption { margin-top:1rem; max-width:70ch; color:var(--muted); font-size:0.95rem; line-height:1.5; }
  .moves { margin-top:clamp(2rem, 3.5vw, 3rem); }
  .move p { font-size:clamp(1rem, 1.1vw, 1.12rem); line-height:1.55; }
  @media (max-width:820px) { .moves { grid-template-columns:1fr; } }
</style>
